  </ul>
  <p>To sell Notion templates on Gumroad, publish your template page in Notion with duplication turned on, then put that link in the content of a Gumroad digital product. Your buyers pay on your product page, open the product's content, and duplicate the template into their own Notion workspace.</p>
  <p>Gumroad doesn't copy the template into your buyers' Notion workspaces. Your buyers get the link from you, and they duplicate the template themselves.</p>

  <h3 id="Get-your-templates-link-in-Notion-Nt4kQ">Get your template's link in Notion</h3>
  <p>You need a public link that lets anyone who opens it duplicate your template.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In Notion, open the page you want to sell.</li>
    <li>Open the <strong>Share</strong> menu, and choose <strong>Publish</strong>.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Allow duplicate as template</strong>.</li>
    <li>Copy the link to the published page.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Notion changes its menus from time to time. If these names don't match what you see, check Notion's help pages.</p>
  <p>Before you go on, open the link in a private browser window. Check that the page loads without signing in, and that it has nothing personal on it. Your buyers get a copy of everything on the page.</p>

  <h3 id="Create-the-product-on-Gumroad-Cp7wR">Create the product on Gumroad</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to your <a href="https://gumroad.com/products" target="_blank">Products dashboard</a>, and click <strong>New product</strong>.</li>
    <li>Type a name for your template in <strong>Name</strong>.</li>
    <li>Under <strong>Products</strong>, select <strong>Digital product</strong>.</li>
    <li>Type a price in <strong>Price</strong>, and click <strong>Next: Customize</strong>.</li>
    <li>On the <strong>Product</strong> tab, write a description, and add a <a href="60-adding-a-cover-image" target="_self">cover image</a>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Save and continue</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Your buyers can't open the template before they pay. Use the description and cover images to show what the template does and who it is for.</p>
  <p>Don't put the template link in the description. Anyone can read the description on your product page.</p>

  <h3 id="Add-the-template-link-to-your-products-content-Lk2mV">Add the template link to your product's content</h3>
  <p>Only people who buy the product see what you put on the <strong>Content</strong> tab. Add the link there as a button.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the product, and go to the <strong>Content</strong> tab.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Insert</strong>, then click <strong>Button</strong>.</li>
    <li>In <strong>Enter text</strong>, type what the button says. For example, "Duplicate the template".</li>
    <li>In <strong>Enter URL</strong>, paste your Notion link.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Insert</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Save changes</strong>. When you are ready to sell, click <strong>Publish and continue</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>After they buy, your buyers click <strong>View content</strong> on their receipt, or open the product in their <a href="247-what-your-customers-see" target="_self">Gumroad library</a>. They click your button, and Notion opens your template.</p>
  <p>To see what your buyers see, run a <a href="62-testing-a-purchase" target="_self">test purchase</a> and click the button yourself.</p>

  <h3 id="Tell-buyers-how-to-set-up-the-template-Bs9xD">Tell buyers how to set up the template</h3>
  <p>Type short instructions on the <strong>Content</strong> tab, under the button. Tell your buyers:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>That they need a Notion account.</li>
    <li>To click the button, then duplicate the page into their own workspace.</li>
    <li>What to do first in the template.</li>
    <li>How to reach you if they have a question.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You can also add files, such as a PDF guide. On the <strong>Content</strong> tab, click <strong>Upload files</strong>, then click <strong>Computer files</strong>. See <a href="289-file-size-limits-on-gumroad" target="_self">file size limits</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="Price-your-template-Pr5jH">Price your template</h3>
  <p>After you create the product, you change its price in <strong>Amount</strong> on the <strong>Product</strong> tab. Here are four ways to price a Notion template.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Give it away for free.</strong> Set <strong>Amount</strong> to 0. Gumroad turns on <strong>Allow customers to pay what they want</strong>. Your buyers can enter 0, or pay $0.99 (USD) or more. People who get a free template are your customers, so you can email them when you release a paid one.</li>
    <li><strong>Let buyers pay what they want.</strong> Set <strong>Amount</strong> to the lowest price you accept, and turn on <strong>Allow customers to pay what they want</strong>. To show buyers a price, fill in <strong>Suggested amount</strong>. See <a href="133-pay-what-you-want-pricing" target="_self">"Pay what you want" pricing</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Sell a personal version and a commercial version.</strong> In <strong>Versions</strong> on the <strong>Product</strong> tab, click <strong>Add version</strong> for each one. Give each version a <strong>Name</strong> and an <strong>Additional amount</strong>. Buyers pay the <strong>Amount</strong> plus the additional amount of the version they choose. See <a href="126-setting-up-versions-on-a-digital-product" target="_self">Setting up versions</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Sell several templates as a bundle.</strong> Create each template as its own product. Then click <strong>New product</strong>, select <strong>Bundle</strong>, set one price, and add your templates to the bundle. See <a href="339-product-bundles" target="_self">Product bundles</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Versions can share one template link, or each version can have its own. To give each version its own link, go to the <strong>Content</strong> tab and open the menu that starts with <strong>Editing</strong>. If <strong>Use the same content for all versions</strong> is checked, clear it. Your existing content moves to the first version. Then select each other version, and add its button.</p>
  <p>To offer a discount, create a <a href="128-discount-codes" target="_self">discount code</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="Update-your-template-and-tell-past-buyers-Up8nF">Update your template and tell past buyers</h3>
  <p>When you edit your published page in Notion, new buyers duplicate the updated page. Copies that past buyers already made don't change.</p>
  <p>If your template's link changes, replace the button on the <strong>Content</strong> tab, and click <strong>Save changes</strong>. Past buyers see the new button the next time they open the product's content.</p>
  <p>To tell past buyers about an update, email them:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to your <a href="https://gumroad.com/posts" target="_blank">Emails dashboard</a>, and click <strong>New email</strong>.</li>
    <li>Under <strong>Audience</strong>, select <strong>Customers only</strong>.</li>
    <li>In <strong>Bought</strong>, choose your template product.</li>
    <li>Under <strong>Channel</strong>, keep <strong>Send email</strong> selected.</li>
    <li>Write what changed, and tell buyers to duplicate the template again from the product's content.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Publish</strong>, then click <strong>Publish now</strong>. If your buttons say <strong>Send</strong> and <strong>Send now</strong>, click those.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A new duplicate is a fresh copy. Tell your buyers that what they added to their old copy stays in the old copy.</p>
  <p>You can send emails after you have earned $100 in sales and received a payout. See <a href="169-how-to-send-an-update" target="_self">Send email updates</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="Questions-about-link-sharing-Sh3tZ">Questions about link sharing</h3>
  <p><strong>Can someone get my template without buying it?</strong></p>
  <p>Yes, if a buyer gives them the Notion link. Anyone who has the link can duplicate the template. Gumroad controls who can open your product's content, where the link is. It can't control the Notion link itself.</p>
  <p><strong>How does Gumroad protect the product's content?</strong></p>
  <p>Each purchase has its own content page. If someone opens that page from a different location than the purchase, Gumroad asks for the buyer's email address first. See <a href="42-content-security" target="_self">Content protection on Gumroad</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>What happens when I refund a buyer?</strong></p>
  <p>After a full refund, the buyer can no longer open the product's content on Gumroad. A copy of the template that they already duplicated stays in their Notion workspace. See <a href="47-how-to-refund-a-customer" target="_self">Issuing a refund</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Do license keys stop sharing?</strong></p>
  <p>No. A Notion page can't check a Gumroad license key, so a key doesn't lock a template.</p>
  <p><strong>What can I do if my link is shared?</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Say in your description and your content what buyers can do with the template. For example, personal use only, or use with their clients.</li>
    <li>Publish a new copy of your template in Notion, and stop publishing the old page. Then replace the button on the <strong>Content</strong> tab. Buyers who paid get the new link from the product's content.</li>
    <li>If another site offers your template without your permission, <a href="354-reporting-piracy" target="_self">report it to us</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Can I sell a template that someone else made?</strong></p>
  <p>No. You can sell templates you made yourself. Reselling other people's Notion templates is <a href="155-things-you-cant-sell-on-gumroad" target="_self">not allowed on Gumroad</a>.</p>
